[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One thing I\'ve learned after more than 20 years shooting commercials, documentaries, and corporate productions is that the best gear rarely impresses me because of what it can do. It impresses me because I stop thinking about it all together.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s exactly what happened the first day I used the Godox Light Mons LE300Bi. Instead of spending my time thinking about the light, I was thinking about my'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'client, my framing, my audio, the interview, all the things that actually matter on a production. And honestly, I think that\'s one of the highest'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compliments I can give any piece of equipment. [music] &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Before we get started, I do want to'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mention one thing. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Godox sent me this light to check out. They didn\'t pay me to make this video. They didn\'t ask to review it before publishing. And they don\'t get to approve anything I'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'say. If I like something, I\'ll tell you. If I don\'t, I\'ll tell you that, too. Now, with that out of the way, here\'s what I found after using this light on a real client production.'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The first time I used the LE300Bi was on'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a corporate interview shoot. I was filming several people in a boardroom who were reading from a teleprompter, and I needed a large soft key light that I could set up quickly without slowing down the day.'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As is often the case, my lighting setup was incredibly simple. I mounted the light onto a stand. I mounted a large umbrella directly into the built-in umbrella holder,'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plugged in a single power cable, turned it on, and I was ready to shoot. That simplicity is something I appreciate more and more every year. The longer I do this professionally, the'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'less interested I become in features, and the more interested I become in workflow. Because when you\'re working by yourself or [snorts] with a very small crew, every extra adapter,'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'every extra cable, every extra power supply, every extra step adds friction. And friction slows you down. One of the things I immediately'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'appreciated about this light is that there isn\'t a separate power supply hanging halfway down the light stand. Everything is built into the fixture'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'itself. There\'s one power cord. It plugs into the wall. It plugs into the light. Done. That does make the fixture a little heavier than some other lights in this'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'category. If you\'re comparing lights strictly by weight, there are definitely smaller and lighter options available. But personally, I\'ll happily accept a'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little more weight if it means I don\'t have another power brick dangling from a stand or another cable to manage. For the way I work, that\'s a trade-off I\'m more than happy to make.'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Another thing I really appreciated is the built-in umbrella holder. That probably won\'t make the front page of a spec sheet, but for me, it\'s one of my favorite features.'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Most of my interviews are lit with large umbrellas. They\'re quick to set up. They\'re lightweight. They\'re inexpensive, and they create beautiful soft, light. With several of my other COB lights,'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I first have to attach an additional adapter just so I have somewhere to mount the umbrella. It\'s not the end of the world, but it\'s another piece of equipment I have to remember to pack,'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remember to bring, and remember not to lose. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; With this light,'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I mounted it on the stand, inserted the umbrella, and I was ready to go. Simple. Another thing I noticed almost immediately was the control layout. Brightness and color temperature are'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'displayed right next to each other. That sounds like a tiny detail, but when you\'re moving quickly, it\'s nice to glance at the fixture and immediately know exactly where your settings are.'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Even better, when I powered the light off and turned it back on later, it remembered my previous settings. Again, small detail, but if you\'ve used lights that reset'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'themselves every time they\'re powered off, you know how surprisingly frustrating that becomes over the course of a long production day. One thing I found myself thinking about while using this light is just how'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spoiled we\'ve become as filmmakers. Modern lighting is incredible. Wireless control, Bluetooth, NFC pairing, special effects, firmware updates, app control.'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s an amazing amount of technology packed into lights today. And if those features are important to your workflow, that\'s great. Personally, I don\'t use most of them. Not because they aren\'t useful,'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they absolutely are. They just aren\'t how I work. When I\'m on location, my phone usually being used for call sheets, production notes,'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'client communications, interview questions, shooting behind the scenes clips, or a dozen other things. When I need to adjust my light, I\'d honestly rather just walk over, turn one knob, and get right back to work.'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Speaking of work, this light had more'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than enough output for the type of productions I typically shoot. On this particular corporate interview, I didn\'t need to push it beyond about 40% brightness. That\'s worth mentioning because I think'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we can sometimes get caught up chasing bigger numbers. But for interviews like this, 300 W was plenty. And despite running it throughout the day, I never once noticed fan noise'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'during the interviews, which I think is also worth talking about, especially because I had it positioned very close to the microphone, as you can see here. Now, I\'ve been using an Aputure 300D as'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one of my key lights for years. It\'s been incredibly reliable and has produced beautiful light on countless productions. In fact, if you\'ve watched some of my older'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'videos, you\'ve probably seen me using it. But it\'s daylight balanced only. More and more, I find myself needing the flexibility of bi-color lighting without having to think about gels or'

[07:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compromises. Since I\'m almost always shooting in rooms with mixed lighting or in rooms where I can\'t turn off the overheads, bi-color lights that give me the ability to match them to other lights has become'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an absolute necessity. The LE300 by gives me that flexibility while still fitting the kind of simple workflow I prefer. Try to look American.'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; Do you have a pen or something? &gt;&gt; It\'s not going to happen. &gt;&gt; So, is there anything I didn\'t like?'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Honestly, not much. The only thing worth mentioning is that it is a fairly substantial fixture. It feels solid, very well-built. Honestly, it feels more premium than I'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expected at this price point. But that solid construction comes with a little extra weight. Whether that\'s a downside depends entirely on how you work. For me, the convenience of the'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'integrated power supply outweighs the extra weight. For someone else, a lighter fixture might be a higher priority. Neither answer is wrong. It just depends'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on your workflow. And maybe that\'s really the point of this entire video. The longer I do this professionally, the less I care about having the longest feature list, and the more I care about'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'equipment that quietly gets out of my way. Equipment that lets me focus on lighting people, having conversations, directing interviews, and telling better stories. Because at the end of the day, the'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audience never sees the light. They see the person in front of the camera. If a piece of equipment helps me get there faster, with less stress, and less friction, that\'s probably the highest'

[09:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compliment I can give it. After using the Godox LE300 bi on a real client production, I can honestly say it\'ll be staying in my lighting kit.'

[09:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Not because it\'s the newest thing, not because someone sent it to me, but because after using it on a real job, it earned its place. Thanks for watching, and I\'ll see you on the next shoot.'
